### Function Name: `format_time_duration`

This function takes an integer representing seconds and returns a human-readable string format (e.g., "3 hours, 20 minutes").

```python
def format_time_duration(seconds):
    """Format seconds into a human-readable time duration."""
    if seconds < 60:
        return f"{seconds} seconds"
    minutes = seconds // 60
    seconds %= 60
    hours = minutes // 60
    minutes %= 60
    days = hours // 24
    hours %= 24
    parts = []
    if days: parts.append(f"{days} day{'s' if days > 1 else ''}")
    if hours: parts.append(f"{hours} hour{'s' if hours > 1 else ''}")
    if minutes: parts.append(f"{minutes} minute{'s' if minutes > 1 else ''}")
    if seconds: parts.append(f"{seconds} second{'s' if seconds > 1 else ''}")
    return ', '.join(parts)
```

